  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how information can be encoded and manipulated using short paired line segments placed in spaces of different dimensionality. A simple red–blue segment appears first as the basic unit, then many such units are shown randomly distributed, suggesting a disordered collection of writable elements.\n\nThe central part compares how these elements can be arranged on regular lattices in 1D, 2D, and 3D. In one dimension, only a few positions exist along a line. In two dimensions, the same type of red/blue feature can be placed across a square grid, increasing the number of accessible sites and orientations. In three dimensions, a cubic lattice provides many more possible locations throughout a volume, with the colored segments embedded along different directions inside the crystal-like structure.\n\nThe right side emphasizes optical control of the 3D array. Arrows labeled with light, \\(h\\nu\\), and the phrases “Write command” and “Read command” indicate that illumination is used first to set the pattern of red and blue segments and then to retrieve it. Overall, the figure conveys a transition from isolated units to dense multidimensional lattices, highlighting 3D optical data storage or programmable volumetric memory.",
